d77crux-cache places verified CRUX binary packages in the official port
directories (core, opt, xorg). pkgmk then considers those ports up to date,
so `prt-get update`/`sysup` (and OctoCRUX) install the binary instead of
compiling. prt-get still decides everything. Ports whose version differs
from the binary snapshot, ports with a locally edited Pkgfile, and ports in
other collections are built as usual.

Once, in /etc/pkgmk.conf (sysvd77 installs already have it):

    PKGMK_COMPRESSION_MODE="xz"

Then, whenever you update:

    ports -u
    d77crux-cache          # -n to only report what it would place
    prt-get sysup

d77crux-cache refuses to run if pkgmk would not build xz packages. It also
refuses if CFLAGS differ from CRUX's default (the binaries are generic,
built with "-O2 -march=x86-64 -pipe"), unless you set ACCEPT_GENERIC=yes in
/etc/d77crux-cache.conf: then the cached ports are generic and everything
else still builds with your CFLAGS. Do not change your CFLAGS for this tool. With a fixed PKGMK_PACKAGE_DIR (for example
/usr/ports/packages) the archives go there instead of the port directories;
an archive already there that d77crux-cache did not place, such as one you
built, is left alone and that package is built as usual.

The binaries come byte for byte from Jaeger's updated CRUX ISO
(crux.ninja). Their manifest and the channel pointer are signed with the
key installed at /usr/share/d77crux-cache/d77crux-binpkg.pub. See binpkg/ in
the d77crux repository.
